.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH MKFS 1M
.SH NAME
mkfs \- construct a file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/mkfs
special nblocks [ ninodes ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Mkfs
constructs a file system on the special file
.I special
by writing a superblock, an empty i-list and a free list onto it, and
leaves it holding one empty directory: its root.
.I Nblocks
is the size of the file system to make.
.PP
.I Ninodes
is the number of i-nodes to leave room for.
It is rounded up to fill the last block of the i-list,
and defaults to one i-node per two blocks \-
1024 of them on a whole drive, in 32 blocks, or 1.6% of the volume.
v7's ratio was one per four blocks and 4.xBSD's one per sixteen; neither transfers.
A block here is 3072 bytes, six times a PDP\-11's,
so a file that cost six blocks there costs one here
while still costing exactly one i-node,
and the natural ratio is nearer 1:1 than 1:4.
.PP
.B "Note:"
.B "the prototype-file form is not ported."
v7's
.I mkfs
took either a proto file \- a bootstrap program, a size, an i-node count,
and a recursive listing of the files to put on the new volume, terminated with
.B $
\- or, if that argument was a string of digits, a bare size.
Only the second is here, and this is the one deliberate divergence in the program.
Four reasons:
.IP \(bu 3
the bootstrap token is meaningless on this machine.
The simulator loads the kernel image directly and nothing reads block 0;
there is no
.IR bproc (8)
here and none is planned.
.IP \(bu 3
.IR cfile (),
the recursive half, has a 3072-byte block buffer and a 512-entry indirect block
as automatics \- about 1024 words of frame per directory level, against a stack of
4096 words that nothing checks.
.IP \(bu 3
those two buffers are exactly the ones a raw transfer cannot use.
See
.B "THE RAW DEVICE"
below: a buffer handed to the disk must be a 512-word-aligned static,
which a recursive function cannot have.
.IP \(bu 3
.I b6fsutil
on the build host does the same job better, from a manifest, and is how every
image in this system is in fact populated.
.SH "THE LAYOUT"
.nf
block 0             the bootstrap.  Written by nothing here, and read by nothing.
block 1             the superblock.
blocks 2..isize\-1   the i-list.
blocks isize..      data.
.fi
.PP
The first data block is reported when
.I mkfs
finishes, and is the superblock's
.I s_isize
\- which is that block number and not a count of i-list blocks.
.PP
.B "Note:"
i-node 1 is left unallocated and there is
.B no bad-blocks i-node.
v7 wrote a dummy regular file there holding the blocks its
.IR badblk ()
had rejected; that routine returned 0 unconditionally even on the PDP\-11,
the kernel's
.IR ialloc ()
will not hand out any i-node below the root's in any case,
and the dummy serves only to give
.IR fsck (1M)
something to be puzzled by.
.PP
.B "Note:"
the free list is built plainly, from the end of the volume down, so that the kernel
allocates upwards.
v7's
.I "[ m n ]"
arguments and the rotational interleave they described are gone with the
.I s_m
and
.I s_n
fields, which this port's superblock does not have: the pattern does not survive the
first churn of the free list, and this is not a drive whose latency it would hide.
.SH "BLOCKS HERE ARE 3072 BYTES"
.B "Note:"
.I nblocks
is a
.B filesystem
block \- 3072 bytes, 512 words of 48 bits \- and so is every number
.I mkfs
prints.
One EC\-5052 drive is
.B 2000
of them.
.PP
This is deliberately not the 1024-byte block that
.IR df (1M),
.IR du (1),
.IR quot (1M)
and
.IR ls (1)
report in.
Those programs
.I measure
a filesystem and a reader of theirs should not have to know its block size;
.I mkfs
.I writes
one, and its argument is the superblock's
.I s_fsize
verbatim.
Expect
.IR df (1M)
to report
.B three times
the number
.I mkfs
was given.
.SH "THE RAW DEVICE"
.B "Note:"
.I mkfs
is the first program on this system that
.B writes
a raw device, and a raw transfer here obeys four conditions that v7 knew nothing of:
the buffer must begin at byte 0 of a word, the count must be a whole number of
filesystem blocks, the buffer's word address must be a multiple of 512,
and the seek must be block-aligned.
The first three fail with
.BR EFAULT " or " EIO ;
the fourth is silent and reads or writes the wrong block.
.I Mkfs
therefore has one buffer, aligned at startup, and every transfer names it.
See
.I cmd/mkfs/README.md
in the source tree, and
.I cmd/df/README.md
for the conditions themselves.
.SH "SUPER-USER ONLY"
.B "Note:"
.I mkfs
writes the raw disk, and
.I /dev/rmd0
is mode 0600 because that one node is the contents of every file on the volume.
So this program works for the super-user and for nobody else.
It is deliberately
.B not
set-user-id, and the reason is sharper than
.IR df (1M)'s:
a set-uid
.I mkfs
would let anybody destroy every file on a volume with one command.
.SH WARNING
.I Mkfs
does not know which volume the system is running from, and nothing stops you naming it.
.I "mkfs /dev/rmd0"
destroys the root filesystem while it is mounted.
There is no check to be made: the raw major and the block major are different numbers
and nothing on this machine maps one to the other.
v7 had no guard either.
.SH FILES
.I /dev/rmd1
\- the second drive, and the only volume on this system it is safe to name.
There is one drive per
.I minor
number, bit 5 the controller, bits 4\-3 the group and bits 2\-0 the drive;
there are no partitions.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B "a filesystem needs at least 8 blocks"
and
.B "the i-list does not leave room for any data blocks"
are the two size checks, and both happen before anything is written.
.PP
.B "cannot read block " \fIn\fR
where
.I n
is one less than
.IR nblocks :
.I mkfs
reads the last block before writing the first, which is how it finds out whether the
drive is that big without knowing the drive's size.
An
.I errno
of 5 there means the device refused the block number \- the volume is smaller than
.I nblocks
\- and a count of 0 means the special is an ordinary file that is too short.
.PP
.B "cannot write block " \fIn\fR
.RI "with an " errno " of 14 means one of the four conditions in " "THE RAW DEVICE"
is broken; 5 means the driver refused the transfer.
.PP
.B "out of free space"
cannot happen and means the free list this program has just built is inconsistent.
.PP
The superblock is written
.BR last ,
which is the commit point: a
.I mkfs
that fails partway leaves a volume with no magic number, which nothing will mount,
rather than one that looks plausible.
.SH BUGS
The data area is not zeroed, so a volume made over an old file system still holds
its blocks until they are allocated.
Nothing leaks through the file system \- the kernel's
.IR alloc ()
clears a block on the way out \- but the raw device still has them.
.PP
.B "Note:"
v7's entry here was that there should be some way to specify links.
It went with the prototype language.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
filsys(5), dir(5), df(1M), quot(1M)
.br
.B "Note:"
v7's SEE ALSO named
.IR bproc (8),
which does not exist here \- see the bootstrap block above \- and
.IR fsck (1M),
which is not ported yet.
